{"causes":["The institutionalization of the pharaonic kingship in ancient Egypt necessitated a record of succession for religious and administrative purposes.","The compilation of king lists during the New Kingdom aimed to legitimize current rulers by connecting them to a long, unbroken lineage of predecessors.","The gradual decentralization of power during the late Old Kingdom led to potential periods of instability and short, poorly documented reigns."],"effects":["Neferka's inclusion on the Saqqara King List provides a chronological marker for Egyptologists studying the Sixth Dynasty's succession.","The uncertainty surrounding Neferka's reign highlights the challenges in reconstructing the precise timeline of Old Kingdom pharaohs.","Neferka's enigmatic status encourages ongoing archaeological and textual research into the late Old Kingdom and early First Intermediate Period."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"saqqara-king-list","sourceName":"Saqqara King List","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"neferka","targetName":"Neferka","context":"The Saqqara King List, a New Kingdom document, lists Neferka as a pharaoh of the Sixth Dynasty, providing the primary textual evidence for his potential existence."},{"sourceSlug":"pepi-ii-neferkare","sourceName":"Pepi II Neferkare","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"neferka","targetName":"Neferka","context":"The exceptionally long reign of Pepi II Neferkare likely influenced the brevity and obscurity of any potential successor like Neferka."},{"sourceSlug":"sixth-dynasty-egypt","sourceName":"Sixth Dynasty of Egypt","verb":"OCCURS_DURING","targetSlug":"neferka","targetName":"Neferka","context":"Neferka's potential reign occurs during the Sixth Dynasty of Egypt, a period of transition and increasing decentralization of royal power."},{"sourceSlug":"memphis-egypt","sourceName":"Memphis, Egypt","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"neferka","targetName":"Neferka","context":"As a pharaoh of the Old Kingdom, Neferka's administrative and religious activities would have been centered in or around the ancient capital city of Memphis, Egypt."},{"sourceSlug":"old-kingdom-egypt","sourceName":"Old Kingdom of Egypt","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"neferka","targetName":"Neferka","context":"Neferka is considered a potential, albeit uncertain, ruler within the chronological framework of the Old Kingdom of Egypt."}],"places":[{"name":"Memphis, Egypt","role":"Ancient Capital"},{"name":"Saqqara, Egypt","role":"Necropolis, King List Source"},{"name":"Ancient Egypt","role":"Realm of Reign"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Neferka","type":"Reference article","slug":"neferka"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3874224","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neferka"],"tags":["Political Leaders","political-systems","geopolitical-linkage","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"Neferka was an ancient Egyptian pharaoh of uncertain date and affiliation — likely a ruler of either the late Old Kingdom or the First Intermediate Period (c. 2200–2050 BCE), a turbulent era of political fragmentation and decentralisation following the collapse of the 6th Dynasty and the central power of the Old Kingdom. The name Neferka (Egyptian: 'Perfect is the Ka' or 'Beautiful is the Spirit') was borne by several individuals in ancient Egyptian history, and the identification of the pharaoh Neferka requires careful attention to the specific evidence available — primarily scarabs, seal impressions, and occasional mentions in later king lists.

The Old Kingdom's collapse (c. 2180 BCE) — triggered by a combination of climate change (severe drought and reduced Nile floods), administrative overreach, rising power of provincial governors (nomarchs), and possibly volcanic activity — created the First Intermediate Period (c. 2180–2055 BCE), a time of multiple competing kingdoms and local dynasties in both Upper and Lower Egypt before the Eleventh Dynasty's Mentuhotep II reunified Egypt. The historical record for rulers in this period is fragmentary and often contradictory, making precise chronology and even the identification of individual pharaohs uncertain.

Neferka's place in Egyptian history — whether as a minor ruler of the late Old Kingdom, a local pharaoh of the First Intermediate Period, or a figure from another period entirely — illustrates the challenges historians and Egyptologists face in reconstructing the complete sequence of ancient Egyptian rulers. The Egyptian king lists (Turin Canon, Abydos King List, Saqqara King List) provide the framework for reconstructing this history, but they are incomplete, inconsistent with each other, and sometimes list figures who cannot yet be confirmed archaeologically.","subjects":["Ancient Egypt","Egyptian Pharaohs","Classical Era","First Intermediate Period","Egyptology","Ancient History","Old Kingdom","3rd Millennium BCE"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":4,"significanceNarrative":"Neferka's significance lies primarily in the historiographical challenges he presents, representing the gaps in our knowledge of Old Kingdom succession rather than direct impact. His uncertain existence contributes to our understanding of the decentralization of power preceding the First Intermediate Period.","significanceCategory":"local"},"_editLog":[]}
